00:01:39.148 --> 00:01:41.328
Jazz music objectifies America.

00:01:48.236 --> 00:01:50.236
and it's an odd form that can.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:54.020
give us a painless way of understanding ourselves.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:55.540
Bye.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:07.360
The real power of jazz and the innovation of jazz is that a group of people can come

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:17.490
together and create art, improvised art, and can negotiate their agendas with each other.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:23.830
And that negotiation is the art. Like you'll hear all the time Bach improvised, and he did improvise,

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:28.070
but he wasn't going to look at the second viola and say, okay, let's play Ein Feisty Berg.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:29.980
They weren't, they were not going to do that.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:37.070
that. Whereas in jazz, I could get together, I go to Milwaukee tomorrow, and it'd be three musicians,

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:41.790
I'd walk into a bar at 2.30 in the morning and say, what you want to play, man? Let's play some

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:45.510
blues. Well, all three of us, all four of us are going to start playing, and I might say

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:58.350
Everybody will just start comping and playing and listening, and the bass, you never know what

00:02:58.350 --> 00:02:58.910
they're going to do.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:00.780
That's our art.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:03.480
The four of us can now have a dialogue.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:04.940
We can have a conversation.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.880
We can speak to each other in the language of music.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:23.460
It is America's music, born out of a million American negotiations

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:29.950
between having and not having, between happy and...

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:39.550
sad country and city between black and white and men and women between the old

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:44.230
Africa and the old Europe that could only have happened

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:46.600
in an entirely new world.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:52.720
It is an improvisational art,

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.100
making itself up as it goes along.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:58.580
Just like the country that gave it birth.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:06.570
It rewards individual expression, but demands selfless collaboration.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:14.590
It is forever changing, but nearly always rooted in the blues.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:23.120
It has a rich tradition and its own rules, but it is brand new every night.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:45.220
It is about just making a living and taking terrible risks, losing everything, finding love, making things simple, and dressing to the nines.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:58.630
It has enjoyed huge popularity and survived hard times, but it has always reflected Americans.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:06.740
All Americans, at their best, jazz, the drummer Art Blakey liked to say,

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:10.370
washes away the dust of everyday life.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:16.540
Of all things.

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:29.410
Jazz music celebrates life, human life.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:38.840
The range of it, the absurdity of it, the ignorance of it, the greatness of it, the intelligence of it, the sexuality of it, the profundity of it, and it deals with it.

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:41.570
In all of its, it deals with it.

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:44.570
You know, it's the ultimate in rugged individualism.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:50.340
It's going out there on that stage and saying, it doesn't matter how anybody else did it, this is the way I'm going to do it.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:59.500
When you see a jazz musician playing, you're looking at a pioneer, you're looking at an explorer, you're looking at an experimenter, you're looking at a scientist.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:03.890
You're looking at all those things because it's the creative process incarnate.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.200
The remarkable men and women who created jazz

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:23.870
came from every part of the country, every walk of life.

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:29.960
They could all do something which most people only dream.

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:38.390
create art on the spot a sometime pimp and full-time ladies man from New Orleans

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:46.330
a pianist of startling originality who falsely claimed to have invented jazz but

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:56.320
who really was the first to show that it could be written down pampered son of

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:59.830
middle-class parents who turned a whole orchestra of extraordinary

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:07.100
musicians into his own personal instrument, wrote nearly 2,000 pieces of music for it to play,

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:18.520
and in the process became America's greatest composer, Russian Jewish immigrant's boy from

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:25.080
the Chicago slums, who was taught the clarinet just to keep him out of trouble, but who grew up

00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:27.950
to teach a whole country how to dance.

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:35.920
the troubled daughter of a Baltimore housemaid whose distinctive style of singing transcended

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:43.540
the limitations of her own voice, and routinely transformed mediocre music into great art.

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:51.910
The son of a Pullman chef from Kansas City, Missouri, who came to New York to launch a

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:58.780
musical revolution, proudly led it for a time, and then destroyed himself, 34.

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:04.930
a dentist's difficult son from East St. Louis, Illinois,

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:08.620
whose lifelong search for new ways to sound

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:13.190
made him the most influential musician of his generation.

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:20.670
Then there was the fatherless waif from the streets of New Orleans,

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:26.150
whose unrivaled genius helped turn jazz into a soloist's art,

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:29.780
who influenced every singer, every instrumental,

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:38.300
every artist who came after him and who for more than five decades made everyone

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:43.720
who heard him feel that no matter how bad things got everything was bound to

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:54.640
turn out all right after all and yet who knows very much of what jazz is really

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:59.860
about or how shall we ever know until we are willing to consider

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.880
everything which it sweeps across our path. Ralph Ellison.

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:23.220
People from all over the world came to New Orleans. Pirates, adventurers, gamblers, exiles,

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:29.970
criminals, Frenchmen, Spaniards, Germans, Englishmen, Irishmen, Indians.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.030
Indians, Chinese, Italians, West Indians, Africans.

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:41.180
In the hundreds of tenements in the rear of the front street buildings, there were people

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:44.620
of all nationalities living side by side.

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:48.720
And there was a whole lot of integrating going on.

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:50.860
Danny Parker.

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:59.740
Jazz grew up in a thousand places, but it was born in New Orleans.

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:04.950
which was, in the early 1800s, the most cosmopolitan

00:10:04.950 --> 00:10:08.160
and the most musical city in America.

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:13.780
But New Orleans was also a major center of the slave trade,

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:18.130
still tolerated in a country that had just proclaimed

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:20.700
that all men were created equal,

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:24.250
and the descendants of the human beings,

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:26.280
who were its living currency,

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.840
would eventually create the most American of art forms.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:49.330
jazz. The whole conception of improvisation is a part of all of American life. If you were a slave,

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:53.880
you had to learn how to improvise. You came on the land, you couldn't speak the language.

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:58.260
You had all kinds of foods and stuff you weren't used to eating. You have another whole system to

00:10:58.260 --> 00:10:58.720
deal with.

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.620
If you can't improvise, you're going to be in a world of trouble.

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:06.040
You're not going to be able to survive.

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:13.520
Jazz is about freedom.

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:18.090
It's about a certain kind of liberation.

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:24.820
There have been other people, of course, have been oppressed in the United States

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:26.980
or have gone through brutal treatment in the United States,

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:29.980
but only African Americans.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:35.620
were enslaved. Only African Americans were legally a people who have a legacy and history,

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:45.250
historical consciousness of having been unfree in a free country. Beginning in 1817, slaves in

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:51.590
New Orleans were permitted to sing and dance every Sunday afternoon in a place called Congo

00:11:51.590 --> 00:11:59.980
Square. To the curious whites, who sometimes turned out to see and hear them, the slaves...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.400
music filled with complex percussive rhythms seemed to provide an authentic

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:15.510
glimpse of Africa. But most of the slaves in Congo Square had never seen Africa.

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:21.750
Many were recent arrivals from the West Indies, their music filled with the

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:23.600
infectious pulse of the Caribbean.

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:47.150
Other slaves have been brought to the city from the interior of the American South, bringing

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:52.930
with them work songs, spirituals, and the call and response of the Baptist Church.

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:16.040
the shot man was also home to a unique and prosperous community of free people

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:22.740
who call themselves creoles of color many were the light-skinned descendants

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:26.800
of French and Spanish colonists and their black wives and mistresses

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.580
They identified with their European, not their African ancestors,

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:37.370
and they looked down on the darker-skinned blacks around them.

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:41.060
Some owned slaves.

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:48.680
Many Creole musicians were classically trained

00:13:50.210 --> 00:13:55.150
and prided themselves on being able to play music for every kind of dancing.

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:08.180
There is a mania in this city, the New Orleans Picayune reported in 1838,

00:14:08.620 --> 00:14:10.300
for horn and trumpet playing.

00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:17.900
Citizens of every color and nationality marched to the music of brass bands.

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:29.980
City streets were...

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:40.630
Filled with parades of every kind, weddings, funerals, feast days, and the six to eight-week carnival season that each spring led up to Mardi Gras.

00:14:40.670 --> 00:14:59.970
In the decades before the Civil War, New Orleans had three flourishing opera companies and two full-fledged singers.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.300
symphony orchestras, one white and one Creole.

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:11.220
There was so much music, so much dancing going on,

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:13.810
that a northern visitor called New Orleans

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:17.120
one vast waltzing and gallopating hall.

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:23.800
It's a romantic city.

00:15:25.270 --> 00:15:26.990
The vendors in the streets would sing arias.

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:33.180
These people are really integrated in the way that they live.

00:15:35.470 --> 00:15:42.820
One block you have an Italian family, various types of Negroes, you have some Creoles, you

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:48.230
have Germans, you know, you have everybody all intermingled and they can't escape each

00:15:48.230 --> 00:15:48.410
other.

00:15:50.980 --> 00:15:57.980
And also you had a tradition of wildness in New Orleans, like gambling and people showing

00:15:57.980 --> 00:15:59.840
their behinds in different various ways.

00:16:03.070 --> 00:16:06.370
But you also had a lot of churches and religious fervor.

00:16:06.450 --> 00:16:07.210
You had voodoo.

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:12.050
You know, you have all of these things coming together now.

00:16:12.660 --> 00:16:15.080
And you have people who don't like each other,

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:17.170
but they have to deal with each other

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:21.100
because they're living together and they're sharing this culture.

00:16:21.140 --> 00:16:22.660
They're sharing all of this like gumbo.

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:24.060
You know, everybody's going to eat some gumbo.

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:46.140
New Orleans theaters also featured minstrel music so-called plantation songs written by

00:16:46.140 --> 00:16:52.970
white and black songwriters performed by whites blacked up as blacks and sometimes in later

00:16:52.970 --> 00:16:57.300
years by blacks blacked up as whites playing blacks.

00:16:59.330 --> 00:16:59.970
on the surf.

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:05.100
Minstrelsy seemed simply to reinforce ugly racial stereotypes.

00:17:13.190 --> 00:17:16.790
Minstrelsy was the most popular form of American entertainment

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:21.930
for about 80 years in the United States, beginning in the 1840s.

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:27.380
It produced the first body of serious pop songs,

00:17:27.600 --> 00:17:29.600
Stephen Foster, James Bland, and others.

00:17:30.060 --> 00:17:32.240
Songs that we still, all of us to this day, know.

00:17:34.180 --> 00:17:37.900
It produced a national humor that we all know.

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:39.690
Why did the chicken cross the road?

00:17:40.150 --> 00:17:42.030
Who is that woman I saw you with last night?

00:17:44.060 --> 00:17:46.820
Because you had minstrel troops very much codified,

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:49.420
all doing the same kinds of songs, same kinds of humor,

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:53.240
crisscrossing the whole country, not just into major cities,

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:55.620
but to all kinds of towns, any place where there was a hall

00:17:55.620 --> 00:17:58.950
where they could perform, it was like early television.

00:17:59.190 --> 00:17:59.970
It was the first...

00:18:00.030 --> 00:18:03.390
entertainment form that everybody in the United States knew.

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:06.820
Everybody heard the same songs, everybody heard the same jokes.

00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:10.560
This had never happened before, and it wouldn't really happen again until the movies.

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:21.220
Despite its overt racism, The Minstrel Show was a blend of lively music,

00:18:21.590 --> 00:18:24.610
knockabout comedy, and sophisticated elegance.

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:29.820
A bizarre and complicated ritual in which blacks and whites alike...

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:34.770
would interpret and misinterpret each other for decades.

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:40.160
I think that there's something that was so resilient

00:18:40.160 --> 00:18:43.240
in the black people, and that everyone in America

00:18:43.240 --> 00:18:44.880
could recognize that resilience.

00:18:47.590 --> 00:18:50.800
And even though it was masquerading, farce,

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:53.860
and comedy, and dance, and a form of music,

00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:56.540
and it seemed like it was uncomplimentary,

00:18:57.160 --> 00:18:59.920
actually, it was something centrally American.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:07.950
about it and that was the beginning of a long relationship between blacks and

00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:12.180
whites and black entertainment and white appropriation of it and this strange

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:15.900
dance that we've been doing with each other since really the beginning of our

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:22.880
relationship in America. Close is too deep a story so you have to degrade the

00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:27.230
relationship you have to do degrading things so that you can live with the

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:29.720
tremendous affront to humanity that's slavery.

00:19:30.250 --> 00:19:38.850
was. The first big minstrel hit was written down and performed by a white

00:19:38.850 --> 00:19:45.830
man known as Daddy Rice, who said he'd first heard it being sung by a black

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:51.840
stable hand. Rice named the tune after the man, Jim Crow.

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:11.520
On January 26, 1861, the state of Louisiana seceded from the Union.

00:20:13.070 --> 00:20:17.510
But just 15 months later, a federal fleet steamed up the Mississippi River

00:20:17.510 --> 00:20:22.840
and forced New Orleans, the largest city in the Confederacy, to surrender.

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:29.270
Occupation.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:36.760
signaled a new birth of freedom for the city's Black population and unleashed a burst of

00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:37.560
creative energy.

00:20:40.710 --> 00:20:44.210
It's the fact of the abolition of slavery that made jazz music possible.

00:20:46.480 --> 00:20:51.510
It comes from a consciousness of those who are outside of something, but in the middle

00:20:51.510 --> 00:20:51.890
of it.

00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:59.750
These are people who are American in the realest sense, but they've been denied access.

00:21:00.320 --> 00:21:05.180
to recognition as Americans, but that doesn't alter the fact that they are American and

00:21:05.180 --> 00:21:09.140
the fact that they have access to all of the information that Americans have access to.

00:21:11.080 --> 00:21:18.200
For 12 years after the Civil War, in the period known as Reconstruction, federal troops occupied

00:21:18.200 --> 00:21:24.880
the South, enforcing civil rights and overseeing America's first attempt at integration.

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:29.980
But in 1877, in a corrupt battle...

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:35.900
room deal between northern republicans and southern democrats the troops were

00:21:35.900 --> 00:21:43.640
withdrawn and reconstruction collapsed overnight white rule was brutally

00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:54.110
reimposed sharecropping replaced slavery the Ku Klux Klan was ascendant

00:21:55.090 --> 00:21:56.750
and lynchings became routine

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:05.980
every aspect of daily life for African Americans became segregated

00:22:06.490 --> 00:22:11.520
under a system that someone named for Daddy Rice's minstrel hit, Jim Crow.

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:19.460
For a time, cosmopolitan New Orleans escaped the worst of it.

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:35.750
Suddenly, I discovered that my legs were in a condition of great excitement.

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:39.620
They twitched as though charged with electricity

00:22:39.620 --> 00:22:45.460
and betrayed a considerable and rather dangerous desire to jerk me from my seat.

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:47.560
Gustav Kohl.

00:22:49.370 --> 00:22:53.450
In the 1890s, two new styles of music reached New Orleans,

00:22:54.630 --> 00:22:57.910
two styles without which there could have been no jazz.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:07.360
The first, created by black piano players in the cities of the Midwest, was jaunty, propulsive, irresistible.

00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:16.680
It drew from everything that had gone before, African-American spirituals and minstrel songs,

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:25.630
European folk melodies and military marches, all set to fresh, insistent, syncopated rhythms.

00:23:39.760 --> 00:23:46.090
called Ragtime, and it would be America's most popular music for the next quarter of

00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:46.510
a century.

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:55.020
Spread first by itinerant musicians and then by the sale of sheet music, it was instantly

00:23:55.020 --> 00:23:59.980
popular with young dancers all over the country who loved it all the more.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:03.020
because their parents did not.

00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:06.580
Ragtime has syncopation gone mad,

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:10.800
and its victims, in my opinion, can be treated successfully

00:24:10.800 --> 00:24:13.980
only like the dog with rabies with a dose of lead.

00:24:15.230 --> 00:24:18.590
Whether it is simply a passing phase of our decadent art culture,

00:24:19.490 --> 00:24:23.120
or an infectious disease which has come to stay, like leprosy,

00:24:24.430 --> 00:24:25.690
time alone can tell.

00:24:26.870 --> 00:24:28.070
Edward Baxter Perry.

00:24:41.480 --> 00:24:44.120
About the same time, New Orleans musicians

00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:45.920
began to hear the blues.

00:24:51.160 --> 00:24:53.880
A steady stream of refugees from the Mississippi Delta

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:56.160
was now pouring into New Orleans,

00:24:56.860 --> 00:24:58.580
in flight from Jim Crow laws.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:01.860
people for whom laboring on the city docks

00:25:01.860 --> 00:25:05.020
promised a better life than any they could hope to have

00:25:05.110 --> 00:25:07.770
back home chopping cotton or cutting cane

00:25:07.770 --> 00:25:09.130
for someone else's profit.

00:25:10.420 --> 00:25:12.860
The blues was part of their baggage.

00:25:14.710 --> 00:25:25.980
The blues is about sculpting meaning out of a situation

00:25:25.980 --> 00:25:29.840
that seems to defy your being able to find meaning in it.

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:35.740
Black people, since the end of the Civil War,

00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:36.940
are searching for an aesthetic.

00:25:38.750 --> 00:25:41.900
They're searching for an aesthetic

00:25:41.900 --> 00:25:44.840
that will free them of minstrelsy,

00:25:46.130 --> 00:25:47.630
free them of the burden of minstrelsy,

00:25:47.750 --> 00:25:49.750
free them of the degradation of minstrelsy.

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:56.070
What emerges from that is a form called the blues.

00:25:56.780 --> 00:25:58.990
And it's a very useful form.

00:25:59.050 --> 00:25:59.770
It's elastic.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:01.940
can do a lot with it because it's simple.

00:26:05.540 --> 00:26:13.120
The blues is an utterly American form, built on just three chords, most often arranged

00:26:13.120 --> 00:26:19.620
in 12-bar sequences, called choruses, that allows for an infinite number of variations.

00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:27.760
And it's a form that, in order for you to really be able to pull it off well, involves

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:28.860
more than just technique.

00:26:28.860 --> 00:26:29.960
you have to have a certain kind of...

00:26:30.030 --> 00:26:39.640
feeling with it. So you create this kind of basic language through which and on which

00:26:39.640 --> 00:26:53.180
they can construct all kinds of things. You have to have that blues. It's like the roux

00:26:53.180 --> 00:26:57.430
in a gumbo. If you don't have roux, you don't have gumbo. Now you might have a suit and it

00:26:57.430 --> 00:26:59.970
might be killing, but if you don't have that roux, you...

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:01.060
You cannot have no gumbo.

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:18.290
The blues was the profane twin of the sacred music of the Black Baptist Church, filled

00:27:18.290 --> 00:27:23.730
with call and response, shouts, moans, exhortations, and signifying.

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:39.300
One was praying to God, and the other was praying to what's human, a New Orleans musician said.

00:27:40.460 --> 00:27:47.180
One was saying, oh God, let me go, and the other was saying, oh mister, let me be.

00:27:49.330 --> 00:27:53.310
There was a big difference between having the blues and playing the blues.

00:27:53.770 --> 00:27:56.470
Playing the blues was a matter of getting rid of the blues.

00:27:58.110 --> 00:27:59.570
The lyrics may have been...

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:07.030
tragic in their orientation, but the music was about having a good time, so the music

00:28:07.030 --> 00:28:09.450
was really a matter of stomping the blues away.

00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:21.910
Blues could be about anything, beautiful woman, a mean boss, the devil himself.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:42.950
intensely personal, meant to make the listener feel better, not worse. Each performer was

00:28:43.360 --> 00:28:56.320
expected to tell a story. We're singing out our feelings.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:01.760
Just can't answer back.

00:29:03.620 --> 00:29:06.380
Then we sing or maybe even hum the blues.

00:29:07.830 --> 00:29:11.690
Yes, us, the blues are sicked.

00:29:19.510 --> 00:29:23.250
I sing and is letting my soul sing out.

00:29:31.270 --> 00:29:36.320
Blues are about freedom. Blues are about freedom, you know.

00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:42.600
There's liberation in reality and when they talk about these songs, when they talk about being sad,

00:29:44.340 --> 00:29:50.890
the fact that you recognize, the fact that you recognize that which pains you is a very freeing and liberating experience.

00:29:51.930 --> 00:29:59.430
It's just, it must be strange for other cultures where you spend most of your time trying to pretend like you don't have any of these problems, any of these, you know, situations.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:01.860
When I hear the blues, the blues makes me smile.

00:30:10.220 --> 00:30:19.080
Came along and said, now, our honest experience is nothing like old Black Joe, you know.

00:30:19.300 --> 00:30:28.470
But at the same time, out of our own resources, we can make a life.

00:30:31.850 --> 00:30:36.270
The lyrics that said, I'm going to lay my head on some lonesome railroad track.

00:30:37.600 --> 00:30:41.580
And when the train come along, I'm going to snatch my damn head back.

00:30:44.280 --> 00:30:51.600
In New Orleans, musicians would find a way to deepen the message of the blues by playing it on their horns.

00:30:56.420 --> 00:30:59.920
You have musicians playing their horns. They have all these instruments that are left over.

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:04.880
the Civil War. The military instruments and the trumpets are played in a militaristic style.

00:31:07.820 --> 00:31:13.330
Then all of a sudden instead of playing in a straight military style of a hymn or a beautiful

00:31:13.330 --> 00:31:17.980
melody, now they're imitating the sound of the people in the church singing. They have the

00:31:17.980 --> 00:31:28.050
The vibrato at the end of the note, the shaking notes, it's a do-dee-dee, boo-dee-dee-loo-dee.

00:31:29.450 --> 00:31:29.970
Then the music.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:31.580
it gets another power and feeling.

00:31:33.540 --> 00:31:36.720
In the way that profound things almost always happen,

00:31:37.340 --> 00:31:40.440
a thing and the opposite of that thing are mashed together.

00:31:43.490 --> 00:31:45.850
Now you have the people getting the spiritual sound

00:31:45.850 --> 00:31:48.240
of the church, and they also are getting

00:31:48.240 --> 00:31:49.960
that secular sound of the blues.

00:31:51.160 --> 00:31:54.570
And the musicians who could understand both of those things

00:31:54.570 --> 00:31:57.620
and put both of them in their horn side by side

00:31:57.620 --> 00:31:59.960
so they could represent that angel and that.

00:32:00.090 --> 00:32:02.700
That was the ones that could play.

00:32:12.240 --> 00:32:16.720
Over the next century, the blues would become the underground aquifer

00:32:17.100 --> 00:32:23.440
that would feed all the streams of American music, including jazz.

00:32:35.850 --> 00:32:38.730
There are differences between the colored man and the white man,

00:32:39.380 --> 00:32:42.160
which neither education nor law can abrogate.

00:32:44.170 --> 00:32:47.910
Sit by a Negro's side at a hotel table or a concert hall

00:32:47.910 --> 00:32:52.760
would be in the opinion of the white people to ignore the truth.

